<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-vector-geom-norm-vs-smul">
<title>Norm versus scalar multiplication</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case:
	<ol>

		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-norm-vs-smul-first-smul">
			Compute <m> k \uvec{v} </m>,
			then use that result to compute <m> \norm{k \uvec{v}} </m>.
		</li>

		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-norm-vs-smul-first-norm">
			Separately compute <m> \abs{k} </m> and <m> \unorm{v} </m>,
			then use those results to compute <m> \abs{k} \unorm{v} </m>.
		</li>

		<li>
			Compare your results
			in <xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-norm-vs-smul-first-smul" text="type-local">part</xref>
			and <xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-norm-vs-smul-first-norm" text="type-local">part</xref>.
		</li>

		<li>
			Reflect on the meaning of the different multiplication operations involved in each
			of <xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-norm-vs-smul-first-smul" text="type-local">part</xref>
			and <xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-vector-geom-norm-vs-smul-first-norm" text="type-local">part</xref>.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> k = -3 </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = ( -1,  5) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{v} = ( 3, -15) </m></p>
			<p><m>
				\norm{(k \uvec{v})}
				= \sqrt{3^2 + {(-15)}^2}
				= \sqrt{234}
			</m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> \abs{k} = 3 </m></p>
			<p><m>
				\unorm{v}
				= \sqrt{{(-1)}^2 + 5^2}
				= \sqrt{26}
			</m></p>
			<p><m> \abs{k} \unorm{v} = 3 \sqrt{26} </m></p>
		</li>

		<li><p>
			Since <m> 234 = 9 \cdot 26 </m>,
			the two results are equal.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> k = - 3/2 </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = \left( - \frac{1}{3}, \frac{1}{2}, 1 \right) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{v} = \left( \frac{1}{2}, - \frac{3}{4}, - \frac{3}{2} \right) </m></p>
			<p><m>
				\norm{(k \uvec{v})}
				= \sqrt{ {\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)}^2 + {\left(- \frac{3}{4}\right)}^2 + {\left(- \frac{3}{2}\right)}^2 }
				= \frac{7}{4}
			</m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> \abs{k} = 3/2 </m></p>
			<p><m>
				\unorm{v}
				= \sqrt{ {\left(- \frac{1}{3}\right)}^2 + {\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)}^2 + 1^2 }
				= \frac{7}{6}
			</m></p>
			<p><m> \abs{k} \unorm{v} = \frac{7}{4} </m></p>
		</li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> k = 6 </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = (1,3,-2,4) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li>
			<p><m> k \uvec{v} = (6,18,-12,24) </m></p>
			<p><m>
				\norm{(k \uvec{v})}
				= \sqrt{6^2 + {18}^2 + {(-12)}^2 + {24}^2}
				= \sqrt{1080}
			</m></p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p><m> \abs{k} = 6 </m></p>
			<p><m>
				\unorm{v}
				= \sqrt{1^2 + 3^2 + {(-2)}^2 + 4^2}
				= \sqrt{30}
			</m></p>
			<p><m> \abs{k} \unorm{v} = 6 \sqrt{30} </m></p>
		</li>

		<li><p>
			Since <m> 1080 = 36 \cdot 30 </m>,
			the two results are equal.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
